Yes. After reading the current repo, I would make this the **Cogym v2 convergence project**: turn the trading-specific implementation into a genuinely world-agnostic optimization laboratory, then use **fact retrieval/verification as the first non-trading world that proves the abstraction is real**.

The repo is already halfway there conceptually. `canonical/` is the active codebase, but its actual runtime is still coupled to trading: `TradingWorld`, `MarketPacket`, `Decision` with market-specific outcomes, and `run_world()` all assume a market.   Meanwhile the older `evolution_lab` already has the pieces we want to preserve: dev/validation/secret evaluation, successive halving, proposal provenance, fresh secret batches, per-metric elites and campaign persistence.

The repo’s own Northstar actually anticipated this convergence into generic `core/`, `worlds/`, `evaluation/`, `evolution/`, etc.  So I would **finish that refactor rather than add yet another version directory**.

One concrete bug to fix before we let Hydra learn anything: `PatternStore.record_result()` computes whether a pattern improved versus the matched control, but writes `mean_reward > 0` into the `improved` column instead of the computed `improved` variable. That can mislabel patterns right now.

# COGYM v2 — World-Agnostic Optimization Laboratory

## Mission

> **Cogym searches for cheaper/faster/better computational policies while preserving a hard externally evaluated quality floor.**

Cogym should not be:

```text
a trading framework
an LLM-agent framework
a prompt optimizer
a HydraDB application
```

It should be:

```text
WORLD
  +
CANDIDATE POLICY
  +
OBJECTIVE / QUALITY GATES
  +
EXPERIMENTAL PROTOCOL
  ↓
controlled episodes
  ↓
hidden evaluation
  ↓
selection / mutation
  ↓
validated improvement
```

Trading becomes one world.

Fact verification becomes another.

Later:

```text
model routing
retrieval routing
coding
resource allocation
multi-agent coordination
context compression
tool selection
research workflows
```

can become other worlds without changing the experiment engine.

